abstract = """Chronic kidney disease is a common and clinically important condition in ageing cats, but the available evidence base for interpretable machine learning in veterinary renal medicine remains fragmented. The original proposal planned an early-onset prediction study using the Dog Aging Project and related cohorts. Because participant-level DAP data were not available and no immediately downloadable feline longitudinal EHR cohort matching the proposal was identified, the final study was redesigned as a comparative interpretable-machine-learning investigation across two separate public feline datasets. The first dataset comprised 56 cats with routine clinical and laboratory variables, including creatinine, blood urea nitrogen, SDMA, urine specific gravity, proteinuria, blood pressure, body condition and CKD stage. The second contained 195 repeated urine-metabolomics observations from 41 animal identifiers with Healthy, calcium-oxalate and CKD labels; the source article reported 42 cats, and this discrepancy was retained as a data-quality limitation. Elastic-net logistic regression, XGBoost and Explainable Boosting Machines were compared using animal-level or observation-level cross-validation appropriate to each cohort. SHAP, LIME, native EBM explanations, feature-selection frequency and ablation analyses were used to examine explanatory behaviour. In the clinical cohort, full-marker out-of-fold AUROC ranged from 0.982 to 0.992, but a context-only ablation excluding label-defining renal markers reduced AUROC to 0.880–0.895, demonstrating substantial circularity in the full-marker task. In the metabolomics cohort, grouped five-fold out-of-fold macro-AUROC ranged from 0.681 to 0.718 and balanced accuracy remained approximately 0.50. The findings support interpretable modelling as a useful analytical framework, but they do not establish clinical deployment or early future-onset prediction. The thesis contributes a transparent two-cohort benchmark, a reproducible pipeline, an explicit leakage and circularity analysis, and a practical account of why veterinary machine-learning claims must be constrained by data provenance."""
add_unit(doc, "Abstract", [abstract])
add_unit(doc, "Keywords", ["Feline chronic kidney disease; Interpretable machine learning; Explainable Boosting Machine; SHAP; LIME; Veterinary data science"])

